                3 to "There is no such approval step; the how belongs to the Developers.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-32", "psm-product-owner", "Which may the Product Owner delegate to others while staying accountable? (choose two)", listOf("Ordering Product Backlog items", "Accountability for maximising the value of the product", "Creating and clearly communicating Product Backlog items", "Deciding how the Developers turn items into an Increment"), setOf(0, 2),
            "The Guide lists the work of Product Backlog management: developing the Product Goal, creating and communicating items, ordering them, and keeping the backlog transparent. It says the Product Owner may do this work or delegate the responsibility to others, but remains accountable.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                1 to "The work can be delegated, but the accountability cannot. The Product Owner remains accountable.",
                3 to "How the work is done belongs to the Developers. It is not the Product Owner's to delegate.",
            ),
        ),

        // Scrum Master
        B.q("psm-p4-33", "psm-scrum-master", "What is the Scrum Master's best response?", listOf("Accept, and ask the Developers to size items larger", "Accept, and track hours per Developer to find who is slow", "Help management understand that she is accountable for the Scrum Team's effectiveness, and that a target on a number like velocity can hide the real state of the work", "Decline, and ask to be judged by the number of events she facilitates"), setOf(2),
            "The Guide makes the Scrum Master accountable for establishing Scrum and for the Scrum Team's effectiveness, which she achieves by enabling the team to improve its practices. Velocity is not in the Guide; a target on it rewards inflating numbers and hides the real state of the work.",
            scenario = "A new Scrum Master is told her performance will be judged by whether the team's velocity rises ten percent every Sprint.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Inflating sizes would meet the target while making the numbers meaningless, the opposite of transparency.",
                1 to "Watching individuals undermines self-management; the whole team is accountable for the Increment.",
                3 to "Facilitating events is not the Scrum Master's accountability; the team's effectiveness is.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-34", "psm-scrum-master", "A Scrum Master says her job stops at the edge of her team, and how the wider organisation adopts Scrum is not her concern. What does the Scrum Guide say?", listOf("She is right; the Scrum Master serves only the Scrum Team", "Only an agile coach outside the team serves the organisation", "The Scrum Master serves the organisation too, for example by leading, training and coaching it in its Scrum adoption", "The Scrum Master serves the organisation only by reporting to it"), setOf(2),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Master serves the Scrum Team, the Product Owner and the organisation. Serving the organisation includes leading, training and coaching it in its Scrum adoption.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Guide lists ways the Scrum Master serves the organisation as well as the team.",
                1 to "The Guide gives this to the Scrum Master; it names no separate coaching role.",
                3 to "Reporting is not in the Guide's list; leading, training and coaching are.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-35", "psm-scrum-master", "A Scrum Master says his job is the process only: whether the Increment is valuable or meets the Definition of Done is no concern of his. What does the Scrum Guide say?", listOf("He is right; the Increment belongs to the Developers and the Product Owner", "One way he serves the Scrum Team is by helping it focus on creating high-value Increments that meet the Definition of Done", "He should inspect each Increment and sign it off as Done", "He should write the Definition of Done so the team has one"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide lists helping the Scrum Team focus on creating high-value Increments that meet the Definition of Done among the ways the Scrum Master serves the Scrum Team. His part is coaching and focus, not sign-off.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Guide explicitly includes helping the team focus on high-value Increments that meet the Definition of Done.",
                2 to "Meeting the Definition of Done is the Developers' accountability; the Scrum Master does not sign work off.",
                3 to "The Scrum Team creates the Definition of Done when the organisation has none; the Scrum Master does not write it alone.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-36", "psm-scrum-master", "A director says Scrum Masters are needed only while a team is new; mature teams can drop the accountability. What fits the Scrum Guide?", listOf("He is right; mature teams no longer need a Scrum Master", "The Developers can share the Scrum Master accountability informally and drop it as a role", "Mature teams can replace the Scrum Master with a monthly coach", "The Scrum Team consists of a Scrum Master, a Product Owner and Developers; dropping one means it is no longer Scrum"), setOf(3),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Team consists of one Scrum Master, one Product Owner and Developers, and that Scrum exists only in its entirety. Mature teams still need someone accountable for establishing Scrum and the team's effectiveness.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Maturity does not remove an accountability; leaving parts out means the result is not Scrum.",
                1 to "A Developer may also be the Scrum Master, but the accountability itself stays.",
                2 to "The Scrum Master is a member of the Scrum Team, not an occasional outside visitor.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-37", "psm-scrum-master", "How can the Scrum Master best help?", listOf("Prepare clearer sign-off forms so the meeting goes faster", "Replace the Sprint Review with a recorded demo the stakeholders can watch later", "Help them see the Sprint Review as a working session to inspect the outcome and decide together what to do next, not a gate to releasing value", "Ask the Product Owner to sign the forms instead of the stakeholders"), setOf(2),
            "The Sprint Review is a working session in which the Scrum Team and stakeholders inspect the outcome of the Sprint and decide future adaptations. The Guide says the team should avoid limiting it to a presentation, and that it should never be considered a gate to releasing value.",
            scenario = "Stakeholders treat the Sprint Review as a demo: the team presents slides, the stakeholders sign a form accepting each item, and they leave.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Faster sign-off keeps the wrong purpose. The Review is not an approval step.",
                1 to "A recording removes the collaboration the event exists for.",
                3 to "Moving the signature to the Product Owner still turns the Review into a gate.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-38", "psm-scrum-master", "A Product Owner believes the Product Goal is hers to work out alone and the Scrum Master has no part in it. What does the Scrum Guide say?", listOf("The Scrum Master serves the Product Owner by helping find techniques for effective Product Goal definition", "The Scrum Master writes the Product Goal for her", "The Scrum Master approves the Product Goal before it is used", "The Scrum Master has no part in anything to do with the product"), setOf(0),
            "One way the Guide says the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner is by helping find techniques for effective Product Goal definition and Product Backlog management. The Product Owner stays accountable for the goal.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                1 to "The Scrum Master helps with techniques; developing the Product Goal stays with the Product Owner.",
                2 to "The Guide gives the Scrum Master no approval role.",
                3 to "The Guide lists several ways the Scrum Master serves the Product Owner.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-39", "psm-scrum-master", "What is wrong with this?", listOf("Nothing; the Scrum Master is accountable for the team's effectiveness, so she chooses the changes", "Only that the Product Owner should make the choice", "Nothing, as long as she chooses within the timebox", "The Scrum Team identifies the most helpful changes; the Scrum Master enables the team to improve its practices rather than deciding for it"), setOf(3),
            "At the Retrospective, the Scrum Team identifies the most helpful changes to improve its effectiveness. The Scrum Master is accountable for the team's effectiveness and meets it by enabling the team to improve its practices within the Scrum framework, not by deciding alone.",
            scenario = "After each Retrospective, the Scrum Master decides alone which of the team's ideas will be put into practice.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "Being accountable for effectiveness means enabling the team to improve, not making its decisions for it.",
                1 to "The Retrospective's changes are identified by the whole Scrum Team, not by one member.",
                2 to "The timebox is not the problem. One person deciding goes against a self-managing team.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-40", "psm-scrum-master", "What does the Scrum Guide say about this?", listOf("It is right; the Scrum Master is the team's single point of contact", "The Scrum Master removes barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams and facilitates stakeholder collaboration, rather than becoming a gate", "It is right, as long as the Product Owner also agrees", "Stakeholders should only ever speak to the Product Owner"), setOf(1),
            "The Scrum Master serves the organisation by removing barriers between stakeholders and Scrum Teams, and serves the Product Owner by facilitating stakeholder collaboration as requested or needed. The Scrum Team itself is responsible for stakeholder collaboration.",
            scenario = "The Scrum Master insists that stakeholders may contact the Scrum Team only through her, to protect the Developers.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Guide gives the Scrum Master no gatekeeper role. Shielding the team from distraction is different from cutting it off from stakeholders.",
                2 to "The Product Owner's agreement does not make acceptable a barrier the Guide asks the Scrum Master to remove.",
                3 to "Changes to the Product Backlog go through the Product Owner, but stakeholders work with the whole team, for example at the Sprint Review.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-41", "psm-scrum-master", "A Scrum Master is told his job is to make the team follow the company's existing process, even where it conflicts with Scrum. What should he do?", listOf("Enforce the company process, since the company pays him", "Help people in the team and the organisation understand Scrum, and work to change what conflicts with it", "Quietly adapt Scrum until it matches the company process", "Report each breach of the company process to management"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide says the Scrum Master is accountable for establishing Scrum as defined in the Scrum Guide, by helping everyone understand Scrum theory and practice, both within the Scrum Team and the organisation.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Scrum Master's accountability is to establish Scrum, not to enforce a process that conflicts with it.",
                2 to "Changing Scrum's core to fit an old process covers up problems and limits Scrum's benefits.",
                3 to "The Guide describes helping people understand, not reporting them.",
            ),
        ),

        // Myths and traps
        B.q("psm-p4-42", "psm-traps", "What is the problem?", listOf("Nothing, since every Sprint is still one month or less", "Sprints are fixed-length events meant to create consistency; letting the selected work set the length gives that up", "Sprint length may only change with the Scrum Master's approval", "The Guide requires every Sprint to be exactly two weeks"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide says Sprints are fixed-length events of one month or less to create consistency. Resizing every Sprint to fit the work turns the timebox into a planning variable and removes the steady rhythm of inspection and adaptation.",
            scenario = "At each Sprint Planning, the team sets that Sprint's length to fit the items it selected: two weeks one time, four the next, three after that.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The one-month limit is only part of the rule. Sprints are also fixed in length to create consistency.",
                2 to "The Scrum Master gains no approval power here, and approval would not restore the lost consistency.",
                3 to "The Guide sets no two-week rule; one month or less is the only limit.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-43", "psm-traps", "A consultant's \"Scrum compliance checklist\" lists these items. Which are actually required by the Scrum Guide? (choose two)", listOf("A Sprint Zero before the first product Sprint", "A Sprint Goal for every Sprint", "Velocity tracked every Sprint", "A Definition of Done for the Increment", "A Definition of Ready for every item"), setOf(1, 3),
            "The Sprint Goal is the commitment for the Sprint Backlog and the Definition of Done is the commitment for the Increment, so both are part of Scrum. Sprint Zero, velocity and a Definition of Ready are not in the 2020 Guide.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "There is no Sprint Zero in the Guide. Every Sprint, including the first, should create a usable Increment.",
                2 to "Velocity is a complementary practice some teams use; the Guide never mentions it.",
                4 to "A Definition of Ready is not in the Guide. Items that can be Done within one Sprint are ready for selection.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-44", "psm-traps", "The Scrum Guide requires every Increment to be released to users by the end of its Sprint. Is this statement true?", listOf("True", "False"), setOf(1),
            "The Guide requires a usable Increment that meets the Definition of Done every Sprint; it does not require each one to be released. An Increment may even be delivered to stakeholders before the Sprint ends. When to release is a product decision, not a Scrum rule.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The Guide asks for a usable, Done Increment each Sprint. Usable is not the same as released, and the Guide sets no release rule.",
            ),
        ),
        B.q("psm-p4-45", "psm-traps", "Is this allowed?", listOf("Yes; the Product Owner owns the Sprint Goal and may change it at any time", "Yes, if the Developers agree at the Daily Scrum", "No; no changes are made that would endanger the Sprint Goal, and if it becomes obsolete the Product Owner may cancel the Sprint", "No; the Sprint Goal may only be changed at the Sprint Review"), setOf(2),
            "During the Sprint, no changes are made that would endanger the Sprint Goal. If the Sprint Goal becomes obsolete, the Product Owner has the authority to cancel the Sprint. The goal is not swapped mid-Sprint.",
            scenario = "Mid-Sprint, the Product Owner replaces the Sprint Goal with a new one and keeps the Sprint running. She says Scrum lets the goal change whenever priorities do.",
            whyWrong = mapOf(
                0 to "The whole Scrum Team defines the Sprint Goal at Sprint Planning, and it is the Developers' commitment for the Sprint.",
                1 to "The Developers' agreement does not change the rule: the Sprint Goal holds for the whole Sprint.",
                3 to "The Sprint Goal is set at Sprint Planning for one Sprint; the Sprint Review does not change it.",
            ),
        ),
    )
}
